Arca
====

Arca is an archive manager for AROS. Use its Zune interface to browse,
extract and create archives, or run arca-cli from the Shell. Both programs
use the same archive engine.

The package includes two helpers that Arca starts when needed: unarc-7z
reads 7z, RAR and ZIP with 7-Zip's code; unarc-lha creates LhA archives.

@TARGET@

Mainline ABIv1 packages (PC and Raspberry Pi) require nightly 2026-11-06
or later with stdc.library 0.53.


Installing
----------

Copy the complete Arca drawer to a location of your choice, such as
SYS:Utilities. Drawer names can contain spaces. Keep unarc-7z and unarc-lha
beside Arca and arca-cli. Arca saves preferences in ENVARC:Arca.prefs; it
needs no other installation outside its drawer. To remove a manually
installed copy, delete the drawer and ENVARC:Arca.prefs.

Earlier versions were called Unarc.
It reads Unarc.prefs if Arca.prefs is absent and saves changes as Arca.prefs.
The old preferences are left intact. Existing archive icons that name Unarc
must be changed to Arca. The helper filenames remain unarc-7z and unarc-lha.

The GUI uses Zune and the NList, NListview and NFloattext classes that come
with AROS. If one of them is missing, Arca names it when it starts.

To open archives with Arca from Wanderer, give an archive a project icon
with Arca as its default tool, or set Arca as the default tool for archive
types in Wanderer's preferences. Installation does not change these
associations.


Using Arca
----------

Start Arca from its icon. Choose LhA, ZIP, 7z or tar.gz with the format
button and set the compression with the slider. Drop files or drawers onto
the marked area to pack them. By default, Arca saves the archive beside the
source files. "Separate archives" creates one archive per dropped item.
Drop an archive onto the window to extract it.

Packing starts by counting the files ("Preparing..."), then shows progress.
Cancel stops either step and removes the unfinished archive.

When a job finishes, the result window shows what happened, the destination
and any notes. "Details..." shows all messages and full paths.

"Create archive..." opens a file requester. Choose files, or press OK with
no file selected to pack the current drawer. "Open archive..."
shows an archive's contents first.

If the archive name is already taken, Arca suggests a new one, such as
"Website 2.zip". Return accepts the new name. Use Replace to overwrite the
existing archive. Arca removes the old archive after the new one is complete.
If another program holds it open and prevents removal, Arca keeps it as
"<name>.old" and reports this in the result.

Double-clicking an archive whose default tool is Arca extracts it into a
new drawer beside the archive, then shows the result. To browse its contents
first, change the double-click action in Settings or use the tool type
MODE=BROWSE.

If a file already exists at the destination, Arca shows its name, drawer,
and the sizes and dates of both copies. Return skips the file. Use Replace
to overwrite it.

Settings... has three pages:

- General: choose the startup window and double-click action. You can open
  the destination drawer after a job or close successful results
  automatically. Results with warnings or errors stay open.
- Packing: save beside the source files, choose a destination each time,
  or use a fixed drawer. Choose the name for an archive with several items.
- Extracting: choose the destination, when to create a new drawer and how
  to handle existing files. Arca can ask, skip them or keep both copies.
  Replacing a file always requires confirmation.

In the browser you can select entries and extract only those, extract everything,
or drag the selected entries onto an open Wanderer drawer window. Amiga+A
selects every entry. Several archives dropped at once are extracted one
after another; the progress window lists them and ends with one summary.

During extraction, Cancel keeps completed files and removes the file in
progress. Arca asks before overwriting existing files.


The Shell command
-----------------

    arca-cli <archive>                 extract next to the archive
    arca-cli <archive> TO Work:Out     extract into Work:Out
    arca-cli <archive> LIST            show the contents, write nothing
    arca-cli new.lha PACK <files>      make an archive (.lha, .zip, .tar.gz, .7z)
    arca-cli HELP                      all options, with examples
    arca-cli FORMATS                   what each format supports

Return codes: 0 done, 5 done with notes (something skipped or renamed),
10 partly done, cancelled or stopped by an error, 20 nothing done. Ctrl-C
stops the command; finished files are kept.


Formats
-------

Extracting: ZIP (also ZIP64), 7z, RAR 1.5 to 5, LhA,
LZX, DMS, tar, tar.gz, tar.bz2, tar.xz, gzip, bzip2, xz, ISO 9660 and more.
Creating: LhA, ZIP, tar.gz and 7z.

Multi-volume sets (name.part1.rar, name.rar + name.r00, name.7z.001) can be
opened through any of their volumes. A set with a volume missing is refused
before anything is written, and the message names the missing volume.

Extraction preserves Amiga protection bits and comments stored in LhA and
LZX archives. Arca also stores these attributes when creating LhA archives.


Passwords
---------

Arca uses unarc-7z to read encrypted 7z, RAR and ZIP archives, including
ZipCrypto and AES ZIP files. If filenames are encrypted, Arca asks for the
password before listing the contents. If only file data is encrypted, it
asks before writing any files.

The Shell command hides the password as you type. In scripts, supply it with
PASSWORD. Without a password, encrypted entries are left out and reported.
The password is not saved or logged anywhere.


unarc-7z
--------

unarc-7z is 7-Zip 26.04 (7z, RAR and ZIP handlers only), built for AROS as a
separate program. Arca uses it for:

- every RAR archive;
- encrypted 7z and ZIP archives;
- 7z and ZIP archives in a method the built-in reader lacks (for example
  Deflate64);
- 7z archives split into several files.

Plain 7z and ZIP archives are read by the built-in reader, which is faster.
Without unarc-7z, Arca still reads 7z, RAR and ZIP as far as the built-in
reader can, without passwords, and says so when an archive needs unarc-7z.
An unarc-7z from another Arca release is not used; install both from one
package. Without it, some file times from RAR 4 archives can be off by your
time zone.


unarc-lha
---------

unarc-lha is LHa for UNIX 1.14i in its Amiga version (by Ola Söder), with the
changes included in this package, built as a separate program. Arca and
arca-cli use it to create LhA archives: header level 1 as Amiga LhA writes it, with file
and drawer comments, all protection bits (h, s, p, a and rwed), dates to the
second, and empty drawers. Its archives open with LhA on any Amiga, with XAD
and with LHa for UNIX. "None" stores (lh0), "Normal" packs (lh5).

LhA packing refuses a volume as a whole (choose the drawers and files on it),
a drawer that would contain the archive itself, and a tree with links in it
(LhA would follow them); use ZIP or 7z for those. Without unarc-lha, Arca
can still create ZIP, 7z and tar.gz archives.


Known limitations
-----------------

- Archive files over 2 GB: files over 2 GB inside an archive extract
  correctly, and the volumes of a RAR set are found whatever their size.
  Older SFS versions cannot seek within files over 2 GB. On those versions,
  a 7z or ZIP archive larger than 2 GB cannot be opened.
  Keep such archives under 2 GB, or split them into volumes.
- Names are shown and written in ISO-8859-1, AROS's character set. A name
  with characters outside it is changed, and Arca lists such changes before
  extracting.
- Links stored in archives are not created; they are listed as not
  extracted.
- Disk images in archives (DMS) are listed but not written to a disk.


Licences
--------

Arca's own code is under the MIT licence. It contains libarchive, XAD,
liblzma, zlib, bzip2 and GNU libiconv, each under its own licence; XAD and
GNU libiconv are under the GNU LGPL 2.1, which lets you rebuild Arca with a
changed version of them. unarc-7z contains 7-Zip under the GNU LGPL 2.1, and
its RAR code comes with the unRAR licence restriction: it may not be used to
re-create the RAR compression algorithm.

unarc-lha is under the terms of LHa for UNIX (Licences/LHa-for-UNIX-terms.txt).
They allow it to be passed on only with its source, which is in the Source
drawer together with our changes; a distribution on a disc or other medium
that is not the Internet should tell LHa's authors; and there are conditions
for commercial use. Read the terms before you put unarc-lha on a medium or
into something you sell.

The full texts are in the Licences drawer, and THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES there
lists what is used where.

This release comes from the Arca source tree at the revision named
in the file Revision; the sources of the libraries are the upstream releases
named in THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.
